## Check Your Understanding
1) .java
2) .class
3) It compiles a .java source file into a .class bytecode file.
4) It runs the compiled bytecode using the JVM.
5) It executes Java bytecode on the actual machine, acting as a layer between the bytecode and the operating system.
6) Because the .class bytecode file is only generated once and doesn't update automatically, so any change to the source code needs to go through javac again to produce new bytecode.
7) Bytecode isn't specific to any one machine, so any device with a JVM installed can run the same .class file regardless of its underlying operating system.
